      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To parameterize the line \\(y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4}\\) such that the distance between the point \\(\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}\\) on the line and the point \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is \\(t\\), we need to express the coordinates \\((x, y)\\) in terms of the parameter \\(t\\).\n\nFirst, we note that the equation of the line can be rewritten as:\n\\[ y = \\frac{3x - 5}{4} \\implies 4y = 3x - 5. \\]\nRearranging this equation, we get:\n\\[ 3x - 4y - 5 = 0. \\]\nThe parameterization we seek can be found by expressing \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\) where:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\mathbf{v} + t \\mathbf{d}, \\]\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) is a point on the line, and \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is a direction vector.\n\nTo find \\(\\mathbf{v}\\), we can use the coordinates \\((3, 1)\\) which satisfy the line equation \\(3(3) - 4(1) - 5 = 0\\):\n\\[ 9 - 4 - 5 = 0. \\]\nSo, \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nTo find \\(\\mathbf{d}\\), we need to find a vector that is perpendicular to the direction vector of the line. A direction vector of the line can be found by taking the coefficients of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) from the line equation \\(3x - 4y - 5 = 0\\), which is \\(\\mathbf{d} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\). However, we need to consider the direction vector that is perpendicular to this one. A perpendicular vector to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) (since the dot product of \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}\\) and \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is \\(3 \\cdot 4 + (-4) \\cdot 3 = 12 - 12 = 0\\)).\n\nThus, the direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) is:\n\\[ \\mathbf{d} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}}. \\]",
